  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mGET /api/notifications[39m            │ List for the dropdown                │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mPOST /api/notifications/:id/read[39m  │ Mark one read                        │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mPOST /api/notifications/read-all[39m  │ Mark all read                        │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ [38;5;153mPOST /api/tasks/:id/watch[39m / [38;5;153m/mute[39m │ Explicit override                    │
  ├───────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
  │ task + comment CRUD               │ The substrate                        │
  └───────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘

  Polling hits only [38;5;153m/count[39m every 30s, and [1mpauses while the tab is hidden[0m — otherwise every abandoned tab bills you a
  request every 30 seconds forever. The full list is fetched only when the bell is opened.

  [1mUI:[0m a header bell with an unread badge, opening a dropdown of "Sam assigned you [3mFix login[0m · 2m ago". Clicking
  navigates to the task and marks it read.

  [1mIdentity:[0m pick a display name, persisted in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, plus a user switcher in the header. The switcher isn't a
  nicety — because of actor exclusion, a single user can never generate a notification they'd see, so without it the
  feature is literally undemonstrable.

  [1mError handling:[0m poll failures retry with backoff and stay silent (a background poll must never throw a banner at the
  user); an unknown name creates a user on first use; notifications referencing a deleted task render with the stored
  title from [38;5;153mmeta[39m rather than 404-ing the dropdown.

  [1mTesting[0m — unit tests, TDD, with [38;5;153mresolveSubscribers[39m as the primary target since it holds all the real logic: the
  creator/assignee/commenter union, actor exclusion, mute beating implicit involvement, watch adding a non-involved
  user, reassignment dropping the old assignee. Then [38;5;153memitEvent[39m for fan-out count and rollback behavior, and route-level
  tests over the endpoints. Plus eslint + prettier from the first commit.

  Does this look right? If so I'll write it up as a spec at [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-notifications-design.md[39m
  (uncommitted) for you to review before we plan the implementation.

